{# ============================================================================
   NAKHA SECTION COLOURS — a per-widget palette override
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Every widget in this theme paints itself from the same design tokens
   (--color-text-primary, --color-flame, --color-border …). So redefining those
   tokens on the section's own root element is all it takes to recolour
   everything inside it — the widget's bespoke CSS *and* the shared primitives
   it borrows (.nakha-btn, .nakha-chip, .nakha-badge, .nakha-eyebrow,
   .nakha-card …) — without editing a single existing rule.

   ── Contract ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The caller passes exactly two things:

     scope   the section's CSS selector, e.g. '#nakha-hero-' ~ id
     c       the widget's whole data object (widget.data)

   Every widget exposes the same field names, so the call is one identical line
   at the end of each widget file:

     {% ui "section-colors.njk", scope='#nakha-xx-' ~ id, c=d %}

   ── The switch ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   `colors_enabled` off → this file emits NOTHING, and the section falls
   straight back to the theme palette. That is the revert: the merchant's
   picked colours stay saved and come back the moment the switch goes on again.
   An individual picker left empty does the same for that one surface, so
   nobody has to clear a field to undo it.

   ── Three things that are load-bearing ─────────────────────────────────────
   1. This MUST be included AFTER the widget's own <style> block. Both target
      the same id with identical specificity, so source order decides the
      winner — and `background` in particular is set by the widget on its own
      root, so an earlier block would silently lose.
   2. The flame tints are derived with color-mix() rather than exposed as more
      pickers. A merchant who picks one brand colour should not then have to
      hand-pick its hover, its wash and its line — and if they did, nothing
      would stop the four drifting out of relation to each other.
   3. The mix targets are direction-aware, and NAKHA flipped them. On a dark
      ground "hover" brightens toward #FFF and the wash darkens toward #000.
      On this cream ground both are wrong: a hover that gets lighter reads as
      disabled, and a wash mixed toward black is a muddy brown nobody can put
      text on. So hover mixes toward the CHAR and the wash toward the CREAM.

      The mix partners are the theme's own char/cream, not #000/#FFF — a wash
      made with pure white on a #FFF7EF page is a cold patch you can see the
      edge of.

   4. It writes the --color-appetite-* names, NOT the legacy --color-flame-*.
      Every primitive in the theme reads the appetite tokens; emitting the old
      names here is how this whole feature silently does nothing.
   ========================================================================= #}

{% set c = data.c | default({}) %}
{% set sc = data.scope %}

{% if c.colors_enabled and sc %}
<style>
  {{ sc }} {
    {% if c.color_bg %}
    --color-bg-primary: {{ c.color_bg }};
    --color-bg-secondary: {{ c.color_bg }};
    --color-section-a: {{ c.color_bg }};
    --color-section-b: {{ c.color_bg }};
    {% endif %}

    {% if c.color_card %}
    --color-surface: {{ c.color_card }};
    --color-surface-alt: {{ c.color_card }};
    --color-bg-tertiary: {{ c.color_card }};
    {% endif %}

    {# titles and any strong text #}
    {% if c.color_title %}--color-text-primary: {{ c.color_title }};{% endif %}

    {# body copy and captions ride together — splitting them gives the merchant
       a way to make captions unreadable and no way to notice #}
    {% if c.color_text %}
    --color-text-secondary: {{ c.color_text }};
    --color-text-tertiary: {{ c.color_text }};
    {% endif %}

    {% if c.color_accent %}
    --color-appetite: {{ c.color_accent }};
    --color-appetite-ink: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 84%, #000);
    --color-appetite-solid: {{ c.color_accent }};
    --color-border-focus: {{ c.color_accent }};
    --color-appetite-hover: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 80%, #2C2A28);
    --color-appetite-dark:  color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 62%, #2C2A28);
    --color-appetite-wash:  color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 12%, var(--color-cream));
    --color-appetite-line:  color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_accent }} 32%, var(--color-cream));
    {% endif %}

    {% if c.color_border %}
    --color-border: {{ c.color_border }};
    --color-border-strong: {{ c.color_border }};
    --color-border-hover: {{ c.color_border }};
    {% endif %}

    {# the widget sets this on its own root from the `background` select; this
       block is later in the source, so it wins #}
    {% if c.color_bg %}background: {{ c.color_bg }};{% endif %}
  }

  {# The CTA gets its own pair. It is routinely a different colour from the
     accent that tints links, icons and the highlighted word in the title. #}
  {% if c.color_btn_bg %}
  {{ sc }} .nakha-btn--primary {
    background: {{ c.color_btn_bg }};
    border-color: {{ c.color_btn_bg }};
  }
  {{ sc }} .nakha-btn--primary:hover:not(:disabled) {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_btn_bg }} 80%, #2C2A28);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, {{ c.color_btn_bg }} 80%, #2C2A28);
  }
  {{ sc }} .nakha-btn--outline,
  {{ sc }} .nakha-btn--ghost {
    border-color: {{ c.color_btn_bg }};
    color: {{ c.color_btn_bg }};
  }
  {% endif %}

  {% if c.color_btn_text %}
  {{ sc }} .nakha-btn--primary,
  {{ sc }} .nakha-btn--primary:hover:not(:disabled) { color: {{ c.color_btn_text }}; }
  {% endif %}
</style>
{% endif %}
